:: Himari - The Price of a Hand [bg-sanctuary]
<h1 class="ptitle">THE SANCTUARY</h1>
<<run setup.himari.mark('hand')>>
<<set _hmGuardReply = $characters.himari.quests.guard_reply ?? 'walked_away'>>
<<addmins 40>>
<br />
The whole yard had stopped working. Fuel drums stood where they had been set down, and the workers on the lower deck stood beside them in their chains, and nobody told them to move. Everyone was facing the same way.
<br /><br />
In the middle of the yard stood a wooden crate. Rusk was on his knees in front of it. A guard stood behind him with both hands on his shoulders, and his right arm had been strapped flat across the crate lid with a cargo belt. He was not grinning now. He was talking fast and quietly to nobody, the same few words over and over.
<br /><br />
<center>
	<<image 'people/himari/hand1.jpg'>>
</center>
<br />
Negan had left the bat on his shoulder for once. He had a cleaver in his hand instead, a heavy old butcher's cleaver with tape wound around the handle, and he was turning it slowly so the blade caught the light.
<br /><br />
They had brought her out too. She stood in the open doorway of her container with a guard on each side, in the same clothes she had arrived in, her braid over her shoulder, her eyes on the deck.
<br /><br />
<<if _hmGuardReply === 'told_negan'>>
	<<negan>>
		There he is. Our friend here came to me. Didn't have to. Didn't ask for a thing. That's a man with some sense, and I want everybody to take a good look at him, because he's going to keep both his hands.
	<</negan>>
<<elseif _hmGuardReply === 'stepped_in'>>
	<<negan>>
		There he is. Heard you had to pull this piece of shit off her yourself. Appreciate it, I do. Next time you come to me first. This is my house. In my house, I'm the one who does the pulling.
	<</negan>>
<<else>>
	<<negan>>
		Somebody saw this piece of shit sniffing around my merchandise. Wasn't you, was it? No. You've got the face of a man who minds his own business. That's fine. Today you can mind mine.
	<</negan>>
<</if>>
<br />
He turned to the yard and raised his voice, and it carried all the way up to the watch platforms on the bow.
<br /><br />
<<negan>>
	One rule! I gave you ONE rule! Nobody looks, nobody touches. And Rusk here, Rusk thought the rule was for other people.
<</negan>>
<br />
He walked over to the container. He took her chin in his hand the way he had on the first day, gently, and turned her face toward the crate.
<br /><br />
<<negan>>
	Look, Dolly. Look. This is because of you.
<</negan>>
<br />
She did not understand a word of it. She understood the cleaver. She tried to close her eyes, and his thumb pressed into her jaw until she opened them again.
<br /><br />
Then he walked back to the crate, and he did not make a speech, and he did not wait.
<br /><br />
He swung the cleaver up over his head and brought it down on Rusk's wrist with his whole weight behind it. It did not go through. It stopped in the bone with a wet crack that you felt in your own teeth, and Rusk screamed and bucked against the guard holding him down, and the cargo belt creaked and held.
<br /><br />
Negan put a boot on the crate, worked the blade loose with two short jerks, and swung again.
<br /><br />
The second blow went through and cracked into the wood of the lid. Negan wrenched the cleaver free and stepped back with it hanging from his hand, the blade running red. Rusk's hand lay on its own on the lid, the fingers still curling and uncurling, a few inches from the arm it had belonged to. Blood came out of the stump in hard red spurts with every beat of his heart, across the lid, over the belt, down the side of the crate in streams and into the mud. In the middle of all that red, the ends of the bones showed white.
<br /><br />
<center>
	<<image 'people/himari/hand_cut.jpg'>>
</center>
<br />
The scream went on for a long time. Rusk folded over the crate, his stump clamped to his chest, and the guard behind him let him fall. Somebody in the yard threw up.
<br /><br />
Across the yard, two of Negan's men held her up by the arms. She had not looked away. She could not.
<br /><br />
<center>
	<<image 'people/himari/hand2.jpg'>>
</center>
<br />
Negan wiped the cleaver on Rusk's sleeve and handed it to the nearest guard as if it were a coffee cup.
<br /><br />
<<negan>>
	Somebody get him to the doc. And somebody hose that crate down. We've got work to do.
<</negan>>
<br />
The yard started moving again. Across it, between the two guards, she was still staring at the crate. She had not made a single sound. Her hands were shaking so hard that she had wrapped them in the hem of her shirt.
<br /><br />
Negan stopped beside you on his way back to the gangway.
<br /><br />
<<negan>>
	Come see me tomorrow. I've got a job for a man with two hands and some sense.
<</negan>>
<br />
He clapped your shoulder and went up the stairs, whistling the same tune as the day the truck came.
<br /><br />
<<link 'Leave'>>
	<<goto 'Sanctuary'>>
<</link>>
